  "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x$ and $a=\\pi .$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x \\\\ f(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\pi )=-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\pi )\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\pi )=0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x{tan}^{2}\u2061x+{sec}^{3}\u2061x \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\pi ){tan}^{2}\u2061(\\pi )+{sec}^{3}\u2061(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061x & =-1+(0)(x-\\pi )+\\frac{(-1)}{2!}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\u22ef \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(x-\\pi )^{2}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=\\pi +0.2$ into the above to approximate $\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\pi +0.2).$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}\u2061(\\pi +0.2) & \u2248-1-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\pi +0.2-\\pi )}^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(0.2)^{2} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{25}) \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{1}{50} \\\\  & =-\\frac{51}{50}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
